#[doc("""
What to do about a survivor, in one sentence.

A hint is a fixed template per spell, filled from the site. It is never
generated by a model: two runs over the same survivor must say the same
thing, and a sentence invented about code kalku did not read is a guess
dressed as advice.

Where the site carries the text it changed, the hint quotes it. Where it
does not — a kalku that reports positions only — the sentence degrades to
its general form rather than inventing the missing half.
""")]

import kalku.wire.enums
import kalku.wire.shapes

#[doc("""
What a developer or an agent should do about this survivor.

Only survivors get one: a wekufe that was killed has nothing to act on,
and one that could not compile is the spell's problem, not the suite's.
""")]
pub fn hint_for(site: Site) : String = match site.spell {
  Arm -> arm_hint(site)
  Compare -> compare_hint(site)
  Connect -> connect_hint(site)
  Negate ->
    "No test tells the two branches of this condition apart#{of_enclosing(site)}. Assert what only one of them does."
  Literal -> literal_hint(site)
  Call -> call_hint(site)
  Await -> await_hint(site)
  Supervise -> supervise_hint(site)
  Foreign -> foreign_hint(site)
}

# A survivor is a wekufe tests ran and did not notice, so "no test reaches
# this clause" is not what one says: a test that takes the clause and
# passes on what the next one answers survives just the same. The sentence
# holds for both, and asks for what closes either.
fn arm_hint(site: Site) : String =
  "No test fails without this clause#{of_enclosing(site)}. Assert what only it returns, in a case that takes it."

# The boundary is the whole point of a comparison: the two spellings
# agree everywhere except where the sides are equal.
fn compare_hint(site: Site) : String = match (site.original, site.replacement) {
  (Some(was), now) ->
    "No test tells `#{was}` apart from `#{now}`#{here(site)}. Add a case at the boundary where the two sides are equal."
  (None, _) ->
    "No test tells this comparison apart from a shifted one#{here(site)}. Add a case at the boundary where the two sides are equal."
}

fn connect_hint(site: Site) : String = match site.original {
  Some(was) ->
    "No test has exactly one side of `#{was}` true#{here(site)}. Add a case for each side on its own."
  None ->
    "No test has exactly one side of this condition true#{here(site)}. Add a case for each side on its own."
}

fn literal_hint(site: Site) : String = match site.original {
  Some(was) -> "No test depends on the exact value `#{was}`#{here(site)}."
  None -> "No test depends on this exact value#{here(site)}."
}

fn call_hint(site: Site) : String = match site.original {
  Some(was) -> "No test observes what `#{was}` does. Assert what it returns or what it changes."
  None -> "No test observes what this call does. Assert what it returns or what it changes."
}

# A driver's spell is the tool's, not ours, so the hint says whose it is
# rather than describing a change kalku never saw.
fn foreign_hint(site: Site) : String = match site.original {
  Some(name) -> "No test detects this change (`#{name}`)."
  None -> "No test detects this change."
}

fn of_enclosing(site: Site) : String = match site.enclosing {
  Some(decl) -> " of `#{decl}`"
  None -> ""
}

fn here(site: Site) : String = match site.enclosing {
  Some(decl) -> " in `#{decl}`"
  None -> ""
}

# A wait that nobody observes expiring is a deadline the suite has never
# seen fire. The sentence names the wait rather than the syntax, because
# what a reader has to write is a test that lets it expire.
fn await_hint(site: Site) : String =
  "No test observes what happens when this wait expires#{of_enclosing(site)}. Add one that lets the deadline fire."

# A supervision decision nobody asserts is a failure path nobody has
# looked at: what the system does when this process dies is whatever the
# code happens to say.
fn supervise_hint(site: Site) : String =
  "No test asserts what happens when this process dies#{of_enclosing(site)}. Kill it in a test and say what should survive."
